## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to design a dynamic programming solution for array partitioning with a per-segment objective function. It combines partition DP with range maximum queries and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over array partitions
- Range maximum queries (precomputation table M[a][b])
- Path reconstruction using parent pointers
- Time/space complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartition(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length-1; // 1-indexed array
  const M = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));

  // Precompute range maximums
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    M[i][i] = A[i];
    for(let j=i+1; j<=n; j++){
      M[i][j] = Math.max(M[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for(let t=1; t<=k; t++){
    for(let i=t; i<=n; i++){
      for(let j=t-1; j<i; j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + M[j+1][i] - c;
        if(val > dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct segments
  const segments = [];
  let curr = n;
  for(let t=k; t>=1; t--){
    const prev = par[curr][t];
    segments.unshift([prev+1, curr]);
    curr = prev;
  }

  return {value: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Optimization Opportunities
- Use sparse table for O(1) range max queries with O(n log n) preprocessing
- Apply divide and conquer optimization if applicable
- Consider rolling array optimization to reduce space to O(n)

## Why This Is Asked

Tests translating a DP state into a practical recurrence with non-overlap constraint and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over index i and count t
- Non-overlapping constraint handled by dp[i-2] for taking a domino
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const NEG=-1e15;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=2;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      const v1=dp[i-1][t];
      const v2=dp[i-2][t-1]+A[i-2]+A[i-1];
      if(v2>v1){ dp[i][t]=v2; take[i][t]=true; }
      else dp[i][t]=v1;
    }
  }
  // backtrack
  // ... reconstruct domino starts from i=n, t=k
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How to adapt if dominoes have variable lengths or weights?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests dynamic programming with multiple constraints: non-adjacent blocks, exact block count, and reconstruction requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Disjoint blocks with at least one-element gap between consecutive blocks
- dp[i][t] transitions for skipping elements vs. ending a block
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sum queries
- Backpointers for reconstructing the chosen subarray endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const prefix = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) prefix[i] = prefix[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip element i
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        prev[i][t] = { skipped: true };
      }
      // End a block at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const prevIdx = s >= 2 ? s - 2 : 0;
        const val = dp[prevIdx][t - 1] + (prefix[i] - prefix[s - 1]) - c;
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = { skipped: false, start: s };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    const state = prev[i][t];
    if (state.skipped) {
      i--;
    } else {
      result.push({ start: state.start, end: i });
      i = state.start - 2;
      t--;
    }
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: result.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over multiple segments with a budget and in/out state tracking to allow adjacency.

## Key Concepts
- DP with segment count and in/out state
- Transitions for starting, extending, and ending a segment
- Handling adjacency and at-most-k constraint

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(a, k){
  const n = a.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  // dp[i][t][s] -> max sum using first i elements
  let dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> [NEG, NEG]));
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      for(let s=0;s<2;s++){
        const cur = dp[i][t][s];
        if(cur===NEG) continue;
        if(s===0){
          // skip a[i]
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
          // start new
          if(t<k) dp[i+1][t+1][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t+1][1], cur + a[i]);
        } else {
          // extend
          dp[i+1][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][1], cur + a[i]);
          // end
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = 0;
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++) ans = Math.max(ans, dp[n][t][0], dp[n][t][1]);
  return ans;
}
```

